  "title": "SOUTHERN CULTURE ON THE SKIDS",
  "tickerText": "SOUTHERN CULTURE ON THE SKIDS SOUND LIKE SOMEBODY THREW ROCKABILLY, SURF GUITAR, SWAMP ROCK, COUNTRY, GARAGE PUNK AND OLD-SCHOOL R&B INTO THE BACK OF A BATTERED PICKUP AND DROVE IT THROUGH NORTH CAROLINA AT FULL THROTTLE. FORMED IN CHAPEL HILL IN 1983, SCOTS EVENTUALLY SETTLED INTO THE LONG-RUNNING TRIO OF GUITARIST/SINGER RICK MILLER, BASSIST/SINGER MARY HUFF AND DRUMMER DAVE HARTMAN. MILLER’S INFLUENCES STRETCH FROM SOUTHERN ROOTS MUSIC AND LINK WRAY TO THE SURF AND PUNK SOUNDS HE SOAKED UP WHILE LIVING IN CALIFORNIA, AND YOU CAN HEAR ALL OF IT COLLIDING IN THE BAND’S MUSIC. THEY’RE TIGHT ENOUGH TO BE SERIOUSLY GOOD, BUT LOOSE ENOUGH TO SOUND LIKE A PARTY MIGHT BREAK OUT AT ANY MOMENT. SONGS ABOUT FRIED CHICKEN, DIRT-TRACK RACING, CHEAP MOTELS, FOOD, CARS AND WONDERFULLY QUESTIONABLE SOUTHERN BEHAVIOUR SIT ALONGSIDE GENUINELY KILLER GUITAR PLAYING AND GROOVES BUILT FOR MOVING. ALBUMS LIKE TOO MUCH PORK FOR JUST ONE FORK AND DIRT TRACK DATE HELPED TURN THEM INTO CULT FAVOURITES, WHIL",
